{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And this is the blessing with which Moses blessed.</b> The word ״וְזֹאת״ is entirely superfluous. Also the conversative vav of ״וְזֹאת״ requires explanation. And it appears that it refers to what is written in parashat Devarim (Deuteronomy 1:10–11): ״HaShem your Elohim has multiplied you, and behold, you are this day as the stars of heaven for multitude — HaShem, the Elohim of your fathers, make you a thousand times so many as you are,״ etc., and our Sages of blessed memory said (Sifrei Devarim 1:11): Israel said, 'Moses, are you setting a limit on our blessings?' He said to them, 'This is from myself; and HaShem will bless you as He spoke to you.' If so, Moses had already blessed them with multiplication, and that blessing was from himself, not by virtue of his being a man of Elohim, and that blessing too was before his death, as Rashi explained there (ad loc. v. 3). Therefore it says here ״וְזֹאת״ with the conversative vav — that in addition to that blessing which Moses blessed from himself near his death, this too is the blessing he blessed them with, but not from himself as the first one was; rather by virtue of his being a man of Elohim, for this whole blessing came to him in prophecy from Elohim, and it was not from himself as the first blessing was. And he gave a reason why he was now made a man of Elohim more than for the first blessing: because this was before his death, immediately near his death, more than it was for the first blessing — for in between he taught Israel the entire book of Mishneh Torah; if so, certainly now he was closer to his death than he was at the time of the first blessing. Nevertheless, HaShem, blessed be He, did not single out His unique name upon him, but only with the name Elohim, which is shared with a judge and man. But at his death it says (Deuteronomy 34:5): ״And Moses the servant of HaShem died there״ — for then the unique name was singled out upon him." "Another matter: in the Midrash (Devarim Rabbah 11:1) they said: Moses began his blessings from the place where Jacob left off, as it says (Genesis 49:28): ״And this is what their father spoke to them,״ and Moses began with ״וְזֹאת.״ And it appears that this midrash may be explained in two ways. The first is this: the word ״וְזֹאת״ is in the feminine, for all the songs of this world were spoken in the feminine, for in all of them there is pain; but for the future, in the masculine — as males do not give birth. And Jacob sought to reveal the End, that is, the future, and it was hidden from him; therefore all his blessings were in promises pertaining to this world, from beginning to end. Therefore he sealed the blessings with the word ״וְזֹאת,״ for every promise of this world involves pain. But Moses, who was a man of Elohim and upon whom the Shekhinah rested, therefore began with ״זֹאת״ but did not seal his blessing with ״זֹאת״ — for the beginning of his words were also in promises of this world, since they are necessary to attain through them the ultimate perfection which cannot be attained without them; but the end of his words is in the promise of the World to Come, as he said: ״Happy are you, O Israel.״ And so it concludes in the Yalkut here (תתקסד), that this whole verse speaks of the true bliss of the World to Come: ״Happy are you, O Israel״ — how much is prepared and stored away for you for the World to Come, etc. And likewise what is said: ״And HaShem showed him all the land,״ etc., ״unto the hinder sea״ (34:1–2) — they interpreted it: unto the last day (Sifrei 33:2), that is, the World to Come. If so, Moses was the opposite of Jacob, from whom the End was hidden but to whom it was revealed; therefore he began from the place where Jacob left off — for the end of Jacob's blessings were only promises of this world, but Moses: the beginning of his blessings were promises of this world, but their end was in promises of the World to Come, as stated." "The second is this: that he began from the place where Jacob left off because all his blessings were in the manner of Jacob's blessings, as it says: ״And Israel dwelt in safety alone, the fountain of Jacob״ (Deuteronomy 33:28) — this teaches you that all his blessings were in the manner of Jacob's blessings. And surely he added something in each blessing; therefore it says: ״וְזֹאת הַבְּרָכָה״ — that he added to the blessings of Jacob, which were sealed with ״וְזֹאת״: he blessed them as well with an addition in each and every blessing. By virtue of his being a man of Elohim, the mysteries of wisdom were revealed to him most abundantly — and all that would come to Israel until the last day, all inclusive."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:16:31.597205069Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}